Oh, missed a bit here. That'll teach me to post after BEER.

First of all, I'm not totally convinced that's the overwhelming majority
usage. We see a very biased sampling in rgr.* because we are reading
newsgroups about videogames, where every single reader plays videogames.
But of course there are still large numbers of people in the world who
play actual roleplaying games (myself included) to whom it is immediately
obvious that stats-and-levels don't make a roleplaying game.

Furthermore... I think there's some life left yet in the "game where you
play a role" usage. There's obviously plenty of life in this "a game with
stats and levels" usage popular in computer gaming. But I do not believe
for an instant there's really significant use in this rationalisation we
see in rgr.dev, where you talk a bit about how it's a roleplaying game
because you might identify with the little @, but it turns out to
conveniently mean games with stats and levels. _That_ is even more a
minority usage than the tabletop roleplayers' usage.
